<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-finance-simple-interest" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Simple Interest and Discounts</title>

  <!-- This section is a remix of PCC, Sekhon and Bloom material, and my own original writing. -->

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Solve problems involving simple interest.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Solve problems involving discounted loans.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      It costs to borrow money.  The <q>rent</q> one pays for the use of money is called the <term>interest</term>.<idx>interest</idx> The amount of money that is being borrowed or loaned is called the <term>principal</term> or <term>present value</term>.<idx>principal</idx><idx>present value</idx>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-simple-interest">
    <title>Simple Interest</title>
    <p>
      Simple interest is interest paid only on the original amount borrowed.  In this scenario, the borrower pays a fixed interest rate on the principal for the entire time the money is held.
    </p>
    <p>
      Calculating interest starts with the principal, <m>P</m>, or the beginning amount in your account. This could be a starting investment or the starting amount of a loan. The <term>interest rate</term><idx>interest rate</idx>, <m>r</m>, in its most simple form, is a percentage of the principal.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, if you borrow $100 from a friend and agree to repay it with 5% interest, then the amount of interest you would pay would be 5% of $100. It is important to remember to change the interest rate, <m>r</m>, of 5% into a decimal by moving the decimal point two places to the left before performing any calculation.
    </p>
    <p>
      <md>(0.05)(\$100)=\$5</md>
    </p>
    <p>
      The total amount you would repay is called the <term>future value</term><idx>future value</idx> and would be $105, the original principal plus the interest.
    </p>
    <p>
      <md>\$100+\$5=\$105</md>
    </p>
    <p>
      One-time simple interest as in the above example is only common for extremely short-term or informal loans. For longer-term loans or investments, it is common for interest to be paid on a daily, monthly, quarterly, or annual basis. 
      In that case, interest would be earned regularly. 
    </p>
    <p>
      To see how this works, suppose you borrow $100 from a friend and agree to repay it with 5% interest per year, and you keep the money for 3 years. 
      How much would you need to repay at the end of the loan? The amount of interest you would pay would be 5% of $100 each year for three years, that is:    
    </p>
    <p>
      <md> (0.05)(\$100)(3) = \$15. </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      The future value of the loan would be $115, which is the original principal plus the interest.
    </p>
    <p>
      <md>\$100+\$15=\$115</md>
    </p>
    <p>
      These ideas are summarized in the block below.
    </p>
    <assemblage>
      <title>Simple Interest Formulas</title>
      <idx>simple interest</idx>
      <idx><h>financial formulas</h><h>simple interest</h></idx>
      <p>
        If an amount <m>P</m> is borrowed for a time <m>t</m> at an interest rate <m>r</m>, then the interest <m>I</m> is 
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>I=Prt.</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The amount <m>A</m> owed at the end of time <m>t</m> is given by
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>A =\amp P + I = P + Prt</mrow>
          <mrow> =\amp P(1+rt).</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Glossary:
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p><m>I</m> is the interest</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><m>P</m> is the principal, starting amount, or present value</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><m>r</m> is the interest rate in decimal form</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><m>t</m> is time, where the increment of time (years, months, etc.) matches the time period for the interest rate</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><m>A</m> is the end amount, principal plus interest, or future value</p>
            </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
      Bonds are an example of a simple investment with a regular interest payout. Governments (federal, state, and local) and corporations issue bonds to raise money.
    </p>
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose your city is building a new park, and issues bonds to raise the money to build it. You buy a $1,000 bond that pays 5% simple interest annually and matures in 5 years. How much interest will you earn? What is the future value of the bond?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Each year, you would earn 5% interest, so over the course of five years, you would earn:
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>\$1{,}000(0.05)(5)=\$250</md>
        </p>

        <p>
          When the bond matures, you would receive back the $1,000 you originally paid and the $250 in interest, so we could also put that into a single calculation:
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>\$1{,}000+\$1{,}000(0.05)(5)=\$1{,}250</md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <p>
      Here are a couple more examples.
    </p>
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Ursula borrows $600 for 5 months at a simple interest rate of 15% per year.  Find the interest, and the total amount she is obligated to pay.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The interest is computed by multiplying the principal by the interest rate and the time.

          <md>
            I = \$600(0.15)\left(\frac{5}{12}\right) = \$37.50.
          </md>
          Note that here the rate is per year but the time is given in months, so we convert 5 months to years by dividing by 12.
        </p>
        <p>
          The total amount is thus
        </p>
        <p>
          <md> A = P + I = \$600 + \$37.50 = \$637.50.</md>
          This total can be computed directly using
          <md>
            <mrow> A =\amp P(1+rt) </mrow>
            <mrow> =\amp \$600[1+(0.15)(5/12)] </mrow>
            <mrow> =\amp \$600(1+0.0625)</mrow>
            <mrow> =\amp \$637.50. </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Darnel owes a total of $3,060, which includes 12% simple interest per year for the three years he borrowed the money.  How much did he originally borrow? 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          This time we are asked to find the principal <m>P</m> given the future value.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$3{,}060 =\amp P[1+(0.12)(3)] </mrow>
            <mrow>\$3{,}060 =\amp P(1.36)</mrow>
            <mrow> \frac{\$3{,}060}{1.36} =\amp P </mrow>
            <mrow> \$2{,}250 =\amp P </mrow>
          </md>
          Darnel originally borrowed $2,250.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-simple-interest-discounts">
  <title>Discounts and Proceeds</title>
  <p>
    Lenders sometimes deduct the simple interest from the loan amount at the time that the loan is made.  When this happens, we say the loan has been <term>discounted</term>.  The interest that is deducted is called the <term>discount</term><idx><h>discounted loans</h><h>discount</h></idx>, and the actual amount that is given to the borrower is called the <term>proceeds</term>.<idx><h>discounted loans</h><h>proceeds</h></idx>  The amount the borrower is obligated to repay is called the <term>maturity value</term>.<idx><h>discounted loans</h><h>maturity value</h></idx>
  </p>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Discount and Proceeds Formulas</title>
    <idx>discounted loans</idx>
    <idx><h>financial formulas</h><h>discounted loans</h></idx>
    <p>
      If a loan with maturity value <m>L</m> is discounted for a time <m>t</m> at a simple interest rate <m>r</m>, then the discount <m>D</m> is 
    </p>
    <p>
      <md> D = Lrt. </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      The proceeds <m>R</m> are given by
    </p>
    <p>
      <md>
        <mrow>R =\amp L - D = L - Lrt</mrow>
        <mrow> =\amp L(1-rt).</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Glossary:
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
          <li>
              <p><m>D</m> is the discount (interest)</p>
          </li>
          <li>
              <p><m>R</m> is the proceeds (the actual amount the borrower gets)</p>
          </li>
          <li>
              <p><m>r</m> is the interest rate in decimal form</p>
          </li>
          <li>
              <p><m>t</m> is time, where the increment of time (years, months, etc.) matches the time period for the interest rate</p>
          </li>
          <li>
              <p><m>L</m> is the amount of the loan that must be repaid, or the maturity value</p>
          </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Francisco borrows $1,200 from a bank for 10 months at a simple interest rate of 15% per year.  Determine the discount and the proceeds.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The discount <m>D</m> is the interest on the loan that the bank deducts from the loan amount.
        <md>
          <mrow>D =\amp Lrt </mrow>
          <mrow> =\amp \$1{,}200(0.15)\left(\frac{10}{12}\right) </mrow>
          <mrow> =\amp \$150 </mrow>
        </md>
        Therefore, the bank deducts $150 from the maturity value of $1,200 and gives Francisco $1,050. That is, <md> R = \$1{,}200 - \$150 = \$1{,}050.</md>  Francisco is obligated to repay the bank $1,200.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If Francisco wants to receive $1,200 for 10 months at a simple interest rate of 15% per year, what amount of loan should he apply for?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        In this problem, we are given the proceeds <m>R</m> and are being asked to find the maturity value <m>L</m>. From the problem statement we see that <m>R = \$1{,}200</m>, <m>r = 0.15</m>, and <m>t = 10/12</m>. We must solve for <m>L</m>. Thus
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>R =\amp L(1-rt) </mrow>
          <mrow>\$1{,}200 =\amp L\left[1-(0.15)\left(\frac{10}{12}\right)\right] </mrow>
          <mrow>\$1{,}200 =\amp L(1-0.125)</mrow>
          <mrow>\$1{,}200 =\amp L(0.875)</mrow>
          <mrow>L =\amp \frac{\$1{,}200}{0.875} </mrow>
          <mrow> \approx\amp \$1{,}371.43. </mrow>
        </md>
        Therefore, Francisco should ask for a loan of $1,371.43. The lender will discount the loan by $171.43 and Francisco will receive $1,200.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>
  </subsection>
</section>